WEBVTT

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:07.488
This video walks through the implemented architecture of WasmGPU,

00:00:07.588 --> 00:00:11.318
following the diagram and the sections on this page.

00:00:13.233 --> 00:00:17.389
The diagram groups forty-seven nodes into five subgraphs,

00:00:17.489 --> 00:00:20.450
from the public API to browser resources.

00:00:20.866 --> 00:00:24.512
The public API is where the application creates

00:00:24.612 --> 00:00:28.020
the WasmGPU runtime and its factory surface.

00:00:28.433 --> 00:00:32.399
The WebGPU engine holds the renderer and the interaction

00:00:32.499 --> 00:00:35.885
tools, plus the compute subsystem and its parts.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:39.409
The object and data model holds scenes,

00:00:39.509 --> 00:00:43.688
transforms, objects, the glTF pipeline, and interop.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:47.876
The WebAssembly driver provides heap and frame-arena memory,

00:00:47.976 --> 00:00:50.784
and Rust routines for the engine's hot paths.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.856
Browser resources are the device, queue,

00:00:53.956 --> 00:00:56.476
caches, buffers, textures, and passes.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:01.890
Solid edges carry structure: the runtime creates the factory surface, the

00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:06.144
frame loop drives the renderer, and compute owns its kernels.

00:01:06.566 --> 00:01:08.724
Dashed edges carry data, such as

00:01:08.824 --> 00:01:11.970
transform records flowing to Rust propagation.

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:19.811
WasmGPU.create initializes WebAssembly and the frame arena, then creates the

00:01:19.911 --> 00:01:24.637
renderer with its adapter, device, queue, and canvas context.

00:01:25.066 --> 00:01:28.584
It then builds the compute subsystem on that device, and

00:01:28.684 --> 00:01:31.878
binds Python interop and the scaling service to it.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:35.645
Destroy stops the loop, clears the scaling cache, and

00:01:35.745 --> 00:01:39.026
destroys compute, then the renderer and its effects.

00:01:40.933 --> 00:01:45.443
Each call to render runs four stages. First it stages records

00:01:45.543 --> 00:01:49.750
in the frame arena, and updates dirty transforms in Rust.

00:01:50.166 --> 00:01:53.623
Then it prepares shadows, writes uniforms, and builds

00:01:53.723 --> 00:01:56.978
frustum-culled draw lists for every object family.

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:00.875
Next it packs model matrices in one Rust call, and can

00:02:00.975 --> 00:02:05.577
filter hidden opaque objects with the previous frame's depth hierarchy.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.937
Finally it encodes shadows, GPU sorts, and

00:02:09.037 --> 00:02:11.468
the main passes, then submits them.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:16.854
Transforms live in a structure-of-arrays store in

00:02:16.954 --> 00:02:20.843
WebAssembly memory, with separate arrays for positions,

00:02:20.943 --> 00:02:23.817
rotations, scales, matrices, and parents.

00:02:24.233 --> 00:02:28.747
The frame arena is an eight mebibyte bump allocator. Each reset

00:02:28.847 --> 00:02:33.434
rewinds it and advances its epoch, so stale slices are rejected.

00:02:33.866 --> 00:02:37.697
Heap arenas bump-allocate inside one heap block, and

00:02:37.797 --> 00:02:40.721
their slices expire on reset or destroy.

00:02:41.133 --> 00:02:46.169
Scientific primitives borrow caller-supplied GPU buffers unless

00:02:46.269 --> 00:02:49.431
created with ownBuffers set to true, and

00:02:49.531 --> 00:02:52.937
a GPUndarray destroys only buffers it owns.

00:02:53.366 --> 00:03:00.467
Precision follows the domain: f64 works in CPU and WebAssembly memory

00:03:00.567 --> 00:03:05.789
and in GPU storage, but WGSL has no f64 arithmetic.

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:11.502
The compute subsystem wraps buffers, pipelines, dispatch,

00:03:11.602 --> 00:03:15.130
kernels, ndarrays, scratch pools, and readback rings.

00:03:15.533 --> 00:03:20.763
The scaling service chains those kernels: it extracts finite values, compacts

00:03:20.863 --> 00:03:25.054
them, finds the range, and builds a histogram for percentiles.

00:03:26.966 --> 00:03:32.692
glTF loading reads JSON or binary GLB sources, decodes accessors in

00:03:32.792 --> 00:03:38.344
TypeScript and Rust, and imports the result into scene resources.

00:03:38.766 --> 00:03:43.844
Import is failure-atomic: every acquisition is registered in a transaction,

00:03:43.944 --> 00:03:47.434
and an error rolls everything back in reverse order.

00:03:47.866 --> 00:03:52.279
Interop copies Pyodide buffers into canonical ndarrays,

00:03:52.379 --> 00:03:56.546
validates views over foreign WebAssembly memory, and

00:03:56.646 --> 00:04:00.566
describes WebGPU layouts without scheduling work.

00:04:02.466 --> 00:04:05.632
WGSL lives in five directories, and is

00:04:05.732 --> 00:04:08.468
minified into the bundle as text.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:12.876
A few invariants hold everywhere: frame-arena slices are

00:04:12.976 --> 00:04:16.444
valid only for their epoch, and transforms update

00:04:16.544 --> 00:04:19.210
before draw lists read world matrices.

00:04:19.633 --> 00:04:23.484
Picking and warmup never use occlusion filtering, and shader

00:04:23.584 --> 00:04:27.106
uniform layouts must match the TypeScript packing code.

00:04:29.033 --> 00:04:32.544
To recap: five subgraphs, a four-stage render path,

00:04:32.644 --> 00:04:37.360
explicit memory ownership, kernel-based scaling, and atomic imports.

00:04:38.766 --> 00:04:41.130
Each subsystem is covered in depth by

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:43.594
its own section of the documentation.
